WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.567
This query combines two cleaning steps.

00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:10.577
BTRIM removes boundary spaces. REGEXP_REPLACE then collapses repeated internal whitespace to one space.

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:14.207
The g flag means replace every matching occurrence, not just the first.

00:00:14.207 --> 00:00:21.007
Run the query and compare the raw and normalized names row by row.

00:00:21.007 --> 00:00:26.452
Connect this idea to an operations team aligning timestamps across locations.

00:00:26.452 --> 00:00:31.787
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:31.787 --> 00:00:39.332
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:39.332 --> 00:00:44.687
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:44.687 --> 00:00:50.235
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
